choices={
 'eigen':[('The zero vector','Can the zero vector be an eigenvector?',{'A':'Yes, for every eigenvalue.','B':'No: an eigenvector must be non-zero.','C':'Only for eigenvalue zero.','D':'Only for a singular matrix.'},'B','The non-zero requirement is part of the definition.'),('A zero eigenvalue','What does an eigenvalue zero imply for a square matrix?',{'A':'It has no eigenvectors.','B':'It is an identity matrix.','C':'It is singular.','D':'It has no real entries.'},'C','A non-zero vector is sent to zero, so the matrix cannot be invertible.')],
 'algebra':[('A shared vector','When can eigenvalues λ and μ be multiplied to obtain an eigenvalue of AB by the definition?',{'A':'Always.','B':'When they belong to the same non-zero vector.','C':'Only when they are positive.','D':'Only when λ=μ.'},'B','Use ABv=A(μv)=λμv for a common v.'),('The constant term','What is the matrix expression corresponding to p(x)=x²+3?',{'A':'A²+3A','B':'A²+3I','C':'(A+3I)²','D':'A² with 3 added to every entry'},'B','A scalar constant is represented by that multiple of the identity.')],
 'cayley':[('A singular inverse','A has characteristic polynomial x(x−2)(x+1). Which statement is correct?',{'A':'A⁻¹ exists by Cayley–Hamilton.','B':'A⁻¹=0.','C':'A is singular and has no inverse.','D':'Its determinant is 2.'},'C','The eigenvalue zero makes the determinant zero.'),('The zero identity','In p(A)=0, what does 0 represent?',{'A':'A scalar replacing only the diagonal.','B':'A zero matrix of the same square size.','C':'A zero column vector.','D':'The identity matrix.'},'B','A polynomial in a square matrix is a square matrix of the same size.')],
 'diagonal':[('Column order','If two columns of P are swapped in A=PDP⁻¹, what must also happen?',{'A':'Nothing.','B':'Swap the corresponding diagonal values of D.','C':'Transpose A.','D':'Square D.'},'B','Each column must remain matched to its eigenvalue.'),('A strict endpoint','If one diagonal component of kⁿAⁿ equals (−1)ⁿ, does the matrix tend to zero?',{'A':'Yes, because the sign alternates.','B':'Yes, because it is bounded.','C':'No, that component does not tend to zero.','D':'Only for odd n.'},'C','Boundedness alone is insufficient; every component must converge to zero.')],
 'reconstruct':[('Transport a vector','For B=MAM⁻¹ and Av=λv, which corresponding vector belongs to B?',{'A':'Mv','B':'v for every M','C':'M⁻¹v for every M','D':'The zero vector'},'A','B(Mv)=MAv=λMv, and invertibility ensures Mv≠0.'),('Enough information','Do three eigenvalues alone determine all entries of an arbitrary 3×3 matrix?',{'A':'Yes.','B':'Only if all are positive.','C':'No; corresponding eigenvectors are also needed for reconstruction.','D':'Only if one eigenvalue is zero.'},'C','Different similar matrices can have the same eigenvalues.')],
 'systems':[('A vanishing determinant','If det A=0, which conclusion is justified for Ax=b?',{'A':'There is always no solution.','B':'There is always exactly one solution.','C':'Check the augmented equations for consistency.','D':'Multiply by A⁻¹.'},'C','A singular system can be inconsistent or have infinitely many solutions.'),('Two free parameters','A consistent system in three variables has two independent free parameters. What is its solution set?',{'A':'A unique point.','B':'A line.','C':'A plane.','D':'No points.'},'C','Two independent directions parameterise the common plane.')],
 'mixed':[('Into or onto','Why do two non-zero eigenvalues make their eigenvector plane map onto itself?',{'A':'The determinant of every matrix is positive.','B':'Each target coefficient can be divided by its non-zero eigenvalue.','C':'The plane normal is always a right eigenvector.','D':'The eigenvectors must have length 1.'},'B','This constructs a preimage for every vector in the plane.'),('A least distance','At the nearest point of a line to the origin, what is true?',{'A':'Its position vector is perpendicular to the line direction.','B':'Its position vector equals the direction.','C':'Its parameter is always zero.','D':'Its distance is always 1.'},'A','The derivative of |p+td|² vanishes exactly when (p+td)·d=0.')]
}
